Lessons- THE DEEP DIVE

Now we get into why this case is technically excellent.

1. Public Does Not Mean Harmless

The biggest architectural failure class here is the assumption:

PUBLIC WEBSITE
=
PUBLIC DATA ONLY

That is only true if authorization is designed correctly.

A public-facing Experience Cloud site can expose a guest-user identity that represents anonymous Internet visitors. Salesforce explains that these visitors share a guest-user profile, which determines which objects and fields they can access.

Conceptually:

INTERNET
   ↓
ANONYMOUS VISITOR
   ↓
EXPERIENCE CLOUD
   ↓
GUEST USER PROFILE
   ↓
OBJECT PERMISSIONS
   ↓
RECORD ACCESS
   ↓
FIELD ACCESS
   ↓
CRM DATA

Every arrow represents a trust decision.

2. The Anonymous User Was Still an Identity

This is an important first-principles insight.

Security teams think:

Anonymous = no identity.

Not really.

Applications often assign unauthenticated users a security context:

Guest User

That context has permissions.

If the permissions are too broad:

ANONYMOUS
+
VALID APPLICATION PERMISSIONS
=
AUTHORIZED DATA EXPOSURE

from the application's perspective.

The attacker may not technically bypass authorization.

The authorization itself may be wrong.

3. The Four Layers Salesforce Says Matter

Salesforce describes four layers controlling Experience Cloud access:

LAYER 1
Object Access
     ↓
Which objects exist to this user?


LAYER 2
Record Access
     ↓
Which records can the user see?


LAYER 3
Field-Level Security
     ↓
Which fields can the user read?


LAYER 4
Field Value Masking
     ↓
What values should be obscured?

That gives InfinityGrid a fantastic diagnostic framework.

A safe request should need to survive all four.

Conceptually:

ALLOW =
ObjectPermission
AND RecordPermission
AND FieldPermission
AND AppropriateValueExposure

Not:

Page Is Public
Therefore
Data Is Public
4. Salesforce Had Already Warned Customers

This is one of the hardest facts in the case.

Salesforce published security guidance on March 7, 2026, before McGraw Hill publicly confirmed its incident in April, warning about active exploitation of overly permissive Experience Cloud guest-user configurations.

Salesforce said the threat actor was using a modified version of Aura Inspector to mass-scan public-facing Experience Cloud sites and extract data where guest permissions were overly broad.

That produces a major InfinityGrid question:

Was McGraw Hill's affected environment reviewed after the March 7 Salesforce warning?

We do not assert that it wasn't.

We document the question.

And another:

When Salesforce tells customers active attackers are scanning for a configuration class, does that move remediation from routine configuration hygiene to incident-prevention priority?

Absolutely worth discussing.

5. The Aura Attack Surface

Salesforce specifically says its broader 2026 campaign involved modified Aura Inspector tooling probing the:

/s/sfsites/aura

endpoint exposed by public Experience Cloud sites.

Conceptually:

ATTACKER
   ↓
PUBLIC EXPERIENCE CLOUD SITE
   ↓
AURA ENDPOINT
   ↓
ENUMERATE ACCESSIBLE OBJECTS
   ↓
GUEST USER PERMISSIONS
   ↓
QUERY ACCESSIBLE DATA

Critical evidence warning:

Salesforce documented this technique in the broader campaign. Public evidence reviewed here does not yet establish that this precise endpoint/tool chain was specifically used against McGraw Hill.

That's how InfinityGrid beats sloppy reporting.

We can teach the technical pathway without falsely presenting it as forensic fact for this victim.

6. This Is Not Classic “Hacking”

A conventional breach:

EXPLOIT
↓
CODE EXECUTION
↓
SHELL
↓
PRIVILEGE ESCALATION
↓
DATABASE

This failure class can look more like:

HTTP REQUEST
↓
LEGITIMATE PUBLIC APPLICATION
↓
LEGITIMATE GUEST CONTEXT
↓
OVERLY BROAD PERMISSION
↓
LEGITIMATE API RESPONSE
↓
DATA LEAVES ENVIRONMENT

That is terrifying for defenders because:

nothing has to crash.

no malware has to execute.

no admin account has to be compromised.

no EDR detection has to fire.

The system may simply answer the attacker's request.

7. Authorization Bugs Can Be Quieter Than Exploits

Imagine your SIEM sees:

HTTP 200
HTTP 200
HTTP 200
HTTP 200

Technically:

Success.

Security-wise:

Maybe catastrophic.

This means defenders have to detect not only failed authorization:

403 Forbidden

but suspicious successful access.

8. Data-Access Velocity Matters

A normal public user might query:

1 record
5 records
20 records

A harvesting tool might query:

10,000
100,000
1,000,000+

That's behavioral security.

Conceptual rule:

IF user_type = GUEST
AND objects_queried > normal_guest_baseline
THEN
    throttle
    alert

Better:

IF guest_user
AND unique_records_accessed > threshold
AND request_velocity > baseline * 10
THEN
    BLOCK_SESSION
    ALERT_SOC
9. Guest Users Should Have a Data Budget of Nearly Zero

A powerful architecture principle for InfinityGrid:

Anonymous identities should inherit zero access and earn every exception explicitly.

Salesforce itself recommends starting guest profiles at zero access and granting back only the minimum functionality required.

Think:

DEFAULT
DENY EVERYTHING
      ↓
Website requires Feature A
      ↓
Grant Object A
      ↓
Only Records Needed
      ↓
Only Fields Needed

Not:

Clone Old Profile
↓
It Works
↓
Ship It
10. “API Enabled” Is an Enormous Decision

Salesforce specifically recommended disabling public API access for guest users where it is not required and called that one of the highest-impact mitigations against the campaign.

That should trigger a board-level technical question:

Why can an anonymous visitor programmatically enumerate our CRM at all?

Maybe there is a legitimate reason.

But somebody should be able to answer.

11. CRM Is Not Just a Sales Tool

McGraw Hill is an education company.

Salesforce might be described operationally as:

CRM.

Security should see:

Identity and relationship database.

It can contain:

NAME
EMAIL
PHONE
ADDRESS
INSTITUTION
RELATIONSHIP
ACCOUNT HISTORY
CONTACT ROLE
SALES ACTIVITY

That is incredibly valuable for follow-on phishing.

HIBP confirmed exactly the kinds of identity/contact attributes that can support this abuse.

12. Education Data Has Contextual Value

An email address alone:

john@example.com

has value.

But:

JOHN
+
EDUCATIONAL INSTITUTION
+
PHONE
+
PHYSICAL ADDRESS
+
MCGRAW HILL RELATIONSHIP

can be more useful.

It enables pretexts like:

textbook account issue
instructor access
course activation
digital-learning subscription
administrator verification
procurement request

That's context amplification.

13. Data Minimization Becomes the Next Question

Suppose McGraw Hill needed a person's address once.

The security question becomes:

Why is it still available to this application path later?

Every retained data element creates another potential exposure.

InfinityGrid model:

DATA RISK
=
SENSITIVITY
×
ACCESSIBILITY
×
RETENTION
×
VOLUME

Retention is a security control.

Not merely a compliance setting.

14. “Our Internal Systems Weren't Compromised” Is Technically Important — But Not the End

McGraw Hill emphasized that its internal systems and core databases were not compromised.

Good distinction.

But modern security boundaries look like:

COMPANY
  │
  ├── Internal Systems
  ├── SaaS
  ├── Cloud
  ├── CRM
  ├── Public Portals
  ├── Vendors
  └── APIs

If company data exists there:

the risk exists there.

The defensive boundary follows the data.

