17. Configuration Drift

One of the most important unanswered questions:

Was the dangerous setting originally intentional?

Because many SaaS exposures happen like this:

2019
Feature needs access.


↓
Permission added.


2020
Project evolves.


↓
Permission stays.


2022
Staff changes.


↓
Nobody remembers why.


2024
Data expands.


↓
Same permission now reaches more information.


2026
Attacker discovers it.

No one had to deliberately misconfigure it in April.

Risk accumulates through drift.

18. Permission Review Should Be Continuous

For public SaaS identities:

Quarterly:
What objects can Guest see?


What records?


What fields?


What APIs?


What changed?


Who approved it?

Better yet:

automate the diff.

Yesterday:
Guest_User → Contact.Email


Today:
Guest_User → Contact.Email
Guest_User → Contact.Phone
Guest_User → Contact.Address

That should raise an alert.

19. Attack Surface Management Needs SaaS Configuration

Traditional external ASM scans:

IP
DOMAIN
PORT
CVE
TLS

Modern ASM should also understand:

PUBLIC SAAS PORTAL
GUEST PROFILE
EXTERNAL OBJECT
PUBLIC API
SHARING RULE
FIELD ACCESS

Because McGraw Hill demonstrates that the attack surface can live entirely inside SaaS authorization.

20. Attack Breakers
Breaker 1 — Zero Guest Permissions

Start from nothing.

Grant only required access.

Breaker 2 — Private External Defaults

Salesforce recommends setting external organization-wide access defaults to Private.

Breaker 3 — Disable Guest API Access

If public API access isn't operationally necessary:

turn it off.

Breaker 4 — Restrict Field-Level Access

A public page might need:

Course Title

It probably doesn't need:

Customer Phone
Home Address
Breaker 5 — Rate Limit Enumeration

Programmatic harvesting should encounter friction quickly.

Breaker 6 — Behavioral Analytics

Detect bulk successful reads.

Breaker 7 — Configuration Monitoring

Alert when public identities gain new permissions.

Breaker 8 — Retention Reduction

Data you no longer possess cannot be exposed.

21. Better Architecture

Bad:

INTERNET
   ↓
EXPERIENCE CLOUD
   ↓
GUEST PROFILE
   ↓
SHARED CRM OBJECTS
   ↓
MILLIONS OF RECORDS

Better:

INTERNET
   ↓
PUBLIC EXPERIENCE
   ↓
DEDICATED PUBLIC DATA MODEL
   ↓
MINIMUM FIELDS
   ↓
MINIMUM RECORDS
   ↓
NO DIRECT GENERAL CRM ENUMERATION

Even better:

PUBLIC REQUEST
     ↓
NARROW PURPOSE-BUILT API
     ↓
SERVER-SIDE AUTHORIZATION
     ↓
SPECIFIC RECORD
     ↓
SPECIFIC SAFE RESPONSE

The public application doesn't need broad CRM access at all.

22. Security Boundary vs Convenience Boundary

Application teams may say:

Direct Salesforce access makes development easier.

Security asks:

What happens when an anonymous user discovers the same pathway?

Convenience architecture:

Public App → CRM

Security architecture:

Public App
   ↓
Controlled Service
   ↓
Explicit Authorization
   ↓
Minimal Data

More components.

Smaller blast radius.

23. Why the 13.5 Million Number Matters Differently

The most intellectually honest presentation is:

SHINYHUNTERS
~45M records claimed


HIBP
13.5M unique emails verified


PUBLIC DATA
100+ GB distributed

These are different measurements.

The attacker measured:

rows.

HIBP measured:

unique email addresses.

InfinityGrid should preserve both.

24. Second-Order Risk

The breach is not over when the download finishes.

Released contact information can increase exposure to:

SPEAR PHISHING
VISHING
SMISHING
CREDENTIAL HARVESTING
ACCOUNT RECOVERY FRAUD
BUSINESS EMAIL COMPROMISE
EDUCATION-SPECIFIC IMPERSONATION

Salesforce itself warned that data gathered in these campaigns can be used to enable later targeted social-engineering and vishing attacks.

So:

SAAS EXPOSURE
      ↓
IDENTITY DATA
      ↓
ATTACKER ENRICHMENT
      ↓
SOCIAL ENGINEERING
      ↓
FUTURE IDENTITY COMPROMISE

The breach can become the input to the next breach.

25. Governance Questions

This is where InfinityGrid gets sharp without inventing blame.

Ask:

Who owned the public Salesforce site?
Who owned the guest-user profile?
Who reviewed its permissions?
When was it last reviewed?
Was the March Salesforce warning received?
Who was responsible for responding to it?
Was a configuration audit performed afterward?
Did security have visibility into Salesforce permissions?
Were public CRM access paths part of penetration testing?
Did application owners classify this as production?
Was Salesforce configuration included in attack-surface management?
Could security independently disable public data access?
How was 13.5M people's information reachable through a “limited” exposed data path?

The final question is the important one:

WHO OWNED THE TRUST BOUNDARY?

Not:

Who owns Salesforce?

Those aren't necessarily the same thing.

26. The Bureaucracy Failure Pattern

This is the mature version of the point you're trying to make across InfinityGrid.

SaaS often creates split responsibility:

Salesforce owns platform security


Business unit owns application


CRM team owns configuration


Security owns policy


Privacy owns data


IT owns identities


Vendor management owns contract


Executives own risk

Everything has an owner.

Yet the end-to-end attack path may have none.

That's the real organizational failure pattern worth documenting:

Distributed responsibility can create centralized risk.

